// DRed PHASES 1 AND 2 — THE OVER-DELETE SET OF A RECURSIVE `rel` AND THE
// COMPACT IT FEEDS, AGAINST ORACLES THAT SHARE NO CODE WITH EITHER.
//
// ── WHAT IS UNDER TEST, AND WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT ───────────────────────
// Retraction through recursion is DELETE-AND-REDERIVE: (1) OVER-DELETE every
// fact with ANY derivation through a retracted row, (2) COMPACT the total to
// `old ∖ OD`, (3) RE-DERIVE, which puts back exactly the facts that still have
// an all-survivor derivation. THIS FILE TESTS PHASES 1 AND 2.
//
// There is no re-derive here and NO RETRACTION SURFACE: the emitter's
// `incr_retract_eligible` still refuses every rel-backed query, so
// `tc_apply` / `tc_retract` / `__wd` do not exist and `fail/wql_incr_rel_no_retract.logos`
// still pins that refusal, unchanged. What this file drives is the three
// emitted PRIVATE siblings directly, as a sibling item in the same module —
// which is the whole reason phases 1 and 2 can be proven before any surface
// exists:
//
//   __wql_tc_scc0     (es = ALL,  &mut tot)                    ← the previous total T
//   __wql_tc_scc0_od  (es = DE,   rd = T,  &mut od)            ← PHASE 1a, seed
//   __wql_tc_scc0_odp (es = PRES,          &mut od)            ← PHASE 1b, propagate
//   __wql_tc_scc0_cpt (od, &mut tot, &mut rm, &mut lat)        ← PHASE 2,   compact
//
// ⚠ `rm` — THE NEGATIVE DELTA, AND WHY IT IS AN OUT-PARAM RATHER THAN A COUNT.
// The compact retains survivors at a write index and pops the tail, so the rows
// it drops are OVERWRITTEN IN PLACE: once `_cpt` returns there is nothing left
// anywhere to reconstruct `OD ∩ T` from. The caller's T has been rewritten and
// `od` is a strict SUPERSET in general (it may name rows T never held — the
// refusal cell at the bottom of this file is exactly such an input). A driver
// that folds this relation into per-group aggregate state has to fold DOWN by
// what left and UP by what phase 3 puts back; `__lat` says HOW MANY left, never
// WHICH, so without `rm` the aggregate cells would go silently stale while the
// relation itself stayed right — a defect no assertion about the rel can see.
// `rm` is asserted here as a SET (against the closed-form oracle ∩ T), as an
// exact SEQUENCE on the chain and the diamond, as a PARTITION of the pre-compact
// total with S, and as UNTOUCHED under a refused compact from a SEEDED Vec.
//
// ── WHY THE INTERMEDIATE SETS AND NOT THE ANSWER ──────────────────────────
// ⚠⚠ AN ANSWER ORACLE IS BLIND TO THIS ARC — measured twice, on two builds, by
// two people. Delete phase 1a's `existing-rel ⋈ DE` seed term and the DIAMOND's
// final answer is BYTE-FOR-BYTE CORRECT — identical rows, identical order —
// while `od` goes 2→1, `S` 3→4 and the re-derive count 1→0. Phase 3 repairs the
// over-deletion, so only INCREMENTALITY dies. Every assertion in this file is
// therefore about an INTERMEDIATE SET: what phase 1 computes and what phase 2
// leaves. Nothing here asks the query for an answer.
//
// ── THE SPECIFICATION OF `OD`, DERIVED, NOT COPIED FROM THE ENGINE ────────
// With T = lfp(ALL), DE the retracted edges and PRES = ALL ∖ DE:
//
//     OD = { f ∈ T : SOME derivation of f uses an edge of DE }
//
// For transitive closure a derivation of `(x,z)` is a walk x→…→z, so `f ∈ OD`
// iff SOME walk from x to z in ALL contains a deleted edge. Decompose such a
// walk at its LAST deleted edge: an arbitrary walk in ALL, then that edge, then
// a walk of survivors only. Hence the CLOSED FORM this file uses as its oracle:
//
//     OD = { (x,z) : ∃ (u,v) ∈ DE .  (x = u ∨ (x,u) ∈ closure(ALL))
//                                  ∧ (z = v ∨ (v,z) ∈ closure(PRES)) }
//
// ⚠ THIS IS NOT `closure(ALL) ∖ closure(PRES)`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE POINT.
// OD is a strict SUPERSET whenever a fact has a second, surviving derivation —
// that fact is over-deleted in phase 1 and only phase 3 can bring it back. The
// diamond cell below asserts the strictness by name, which is what makes phase 3
// load-bearing rather than decorative BEFORE a line of it is written.
//
// ── THE ORACLE, AND WHY IT IS AN ORACLE ───────────────────────────────────
// `naive_closure` is repeat-`P := P ∪ (P ∘ E)`-until-no-growth with LINEAR
// dedup: no semi-naive rewriting, no delta, no shadow set, no SCC, no phases.
// `od_oracle` is the closed form above — two closures and a double loop over DE
// — which does not propagate anything and therefore does not share DRed's shape
// either. Every cell additionally carries its answer HAND-COMPUTED in the
// comment above it.
// ⚠ `stdlib/mem/deem/incr_rec.logos`'s `fn dred` is a THIRD WITNESS that runs
// THE SAME ALGORITHM as the emitter, so it is cited nowhere in this file as
// agreement. An oracle that shares the algorithm is not an oracle.
//
// ── WHAT A FAILURE LOOKS LIKE, AND HOW MUCH WORK THE BROKEN CODE DID FIRST ─
// Each cell asserts the FINAL over-delete set, after both phases have run to
// their fixpoints over the whole graph. Nothing is caught at the first push.
//
// THREE CONTROLS WERE RUN, each a REMOVAL OR ADDITION OF A WHOLE EMITTED BODY
// BLOCK in the generated fn — not a flipped condition inside a shared branch, so
// what the artifact differs by is visible in the emitted source. Each was built
// with the archives dropped and the whole stdlib rebuilt.
//
//  • UNDER-DELETION (the answer would come out too BIG after compact). CONTROL,
//    MEASURED 2026-08-07: drop the RECURSIVE bodies from `_od`'s round 0, so the
//    seed is `base ⋈ DE` only and the `existing-rel ⋈ DE` term is gone.
//    → **exit 36** — the CHAIN cell, set mismatch. The chain's (1,4) is derived
//    at DEPTH 2 THROUGH the deleted edge, never FROM it, so the broken seed
//    over-deletes {(2,3),(2,4)} and leaves (1,3),(1,4) standing.
//  • PHASE 1b STOPPING EARLY. CONTROL, MEASURED 2026-08-07: cap `_odp`'s loop at
//    ONE round (the emitted `loop` gains a counter and a `break`).
//    → **exit 56** — the SELF-SUPPORTING CYCLE cell. The chain and the diamond
//    stayed GREEN: their propagation is done in one round. THAT is why the cycle
//    cell is not optional — it is the only one here that walks a cycle in 1b.
//  • PHASE 1b RE-SEEDING FROM THE EDB — i.e. exactly what reusing the insert
//    driver `_i` as phase 1b would do, since `_i`'s round 0 runs every body
//    including the base ones. CONTROL, MEASURED 2026-08-07: run the base bodies
//    in `_odp` too. → **exit 36** — the CHAIN cell: OD gains the surviving base
//    edges (1,2) and (3,4), 6 rows instead of 4. This is the control that
//    falsifies the plan's "1b = `_i` with `__tot := __od`", and the defect it
//    names is one NO final-answer oracle could ever see: it makes phase 2
//    compact the relation to nothing and phase 3 recompute the whole closure —
//    still correct, no longer incremental.
//
// ── PHASE 2's OWN CONTROLS, ALL THREE MEASURED 2026-08-08 ─────────────────
// Each is an edit to the EMITTED body in `emit_scc_od_fns`, built with the
// archives dropped and the whole stdlib rebuilt.
//
//  • THE COMPACT DOES NOT SHORTEN. CONTROL: delete `_cpt`'s `pop` loop, leaving
//    the retained prefix followed by the stale tail the retain never overwrote.
//    → **exit 107** — the chain's `|S|` latch says 2 while the total still holds
//    6 rows. Predicted before running.
//  • THE REFUSAL COMES AFTER THE WRITE. CONTROL: move the `OD ⊆ T` check out of
//    the read-only pass and to the end of the compact, i.e. the one-pass shape.
//    → **exit 99** — the three good cells stay GREEN and only the `OD ⊄ T` cell
//    speaks: the call still refuses, but the total has already been rewritten to
//    `[12,34]` and the rows it dropped are gone. Predicted before running. THIS
//    IS THE CONTROL THAT MAKES "the total is untouched" MEAN SOMETHING — see the
//    note on that cell about why its DE deletes a real edge as well as a
//    never-inserted one.
//  • `rm` IS THE WRONG SIDE OF THE PARTITION. CONTROL, MEASURED 2026-08-08:
//    push into `__rm_<m>` from the SURVIVOR branch (`!__hit2`) instead of the
//    removed one, so the out-param comes back holding `S`.
//    → **exit 112** — the chain's `cell_p2`, at the `rm` set equality (100+12).
//    Predicted before running. `wql_incr_rel_cpt_mutrec_txn` reported 64 and
//    `wql_incr_rel_dred_product_random` 22 under the same control; the phase-3
//    fixture reported 48, from the `|T| − |rm| + |RD| = |closure(PRES)|`
//    arithmetic rather than the check named in its prediction — the DEFECT was
//    predicted correctly and the CHECK-WITHIN-THE-CELL was not, and that is
//    recorded rather than re-fitted.
//  • THE ORDER IS NOT PRESERVED. CONTROL: swap the first two survivors after the
//    compact (written out with `get`/`set`, so the control's own code is under
//    measurement and not a library call). → **exit 111** — `same_set` and the
//    `|S|` latch still agree; ONLY the subsequence check speaks. Predicted
//    before running, and it is the measurement that separates the ORDER
//    assertion from the SET assertion rather than assuming it does.
//
// ⚠⚠ AN EARLIER ATTEMPT AT THE THIRD CONTROL USED `Vec::reverse` AND CAME OUT
// AT **110**, i.e. `same_set` FAILED — but a reversal is a PERMUTATION and
// cannot change a set. The control was therefore rewritten to use an explicit
// `get`/`set` swap (which behaves correctly, hence the 111 above), and the
// anomaly was ISOLATED rather than waved at. MEASURED 2026-08-08, standalone,
// on this build:
//     Vec<i64>::reverse          → correct
//     Vec<StructOfTwoI64>::reverse → correct
//     Vec<(i64,i64)>::reverse    → [(3,4),(3,4)] — the element is DUPLICATED
//     Vec<(i64,i64)>::swap(0,1)  → [(3,4),(3,4)] — same
// So `let tmp: T = self.ptr[i]; self.ptr[i] = self.ptr[j]; self.ptr[j] = tmp;`
// (vec.logos, `swap` and `reverse`) does not COPY when `T` is a TUPLE — `tmp`
// aliases the slot instead — while the identical code is correct for a scalar
// and for a struct of the same shape. THAT IS A DEFECT IN `Vec`/the tuple
// lowering, NOT in anything this file or this slice emits, and it is OUT OF
// FENCE here: it is reported, not fixed and not gated, because fixing it is a
// different change with a different blast radius. Nothing in `_cpt` touches it —
// the compact moves rows with `get`/`set`, which is measured correct above.
//
// exit 0 = on all three cells: the emitted over-delete set equals the closed
// form, the previous total equals the naive closure, OD's strict excess over
// `closure(ALL) ∖ closure(PRES)` is present where the diamond says it must be,
// the compacted total equals `T ∖ OD` as a SET and as an exact SEQUENCE, the
// three latches report `|OD|`, `|S|` and the `-1` "phase 3 never ran" sentinel,
// and a compact whose OD is not contained in T refuses without having moved a
// single row.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.std.wql.el;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct E { pub a: i64, pub b: i64 }

pub deem tc(es: &[E]) {
    rel path(a: i64, b: i64) {
        from es e select (e.a, e.b);
        from path p join es e on p.b == e.a select (p.a, e.b);
    }
    from path p group by p.a aggregate c = count() select (key, c)
}

// ── ORACLE HALF 1: a from-scratch naive closure. Shares nothing with the
// emitter — `P := E ∪ (P ∘ E)` to quiescence, dedup by linear `==`.
fn pair_uniq(p: &mut Vec<(i64, i64)>, a: i64, b: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < p.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = p.get(i);
        if c.0 == a && c.1 == b { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    p.push((a, b));
    return true;
}

fn pair_has(p: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, a: i64, b: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < p.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = p.get(i);
        if c.0 == a && c.1 == b { return true; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return false;
}

fn naive_closure(es: &[E]) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut p: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < es.len() {
        pair_uniq(&mut p, es[i].a, es[i].b);
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    let mut grew: bool = true;
    while grew {
        grew = false;
        let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
        while k < p.len() {
            let pr: (i64, i64) = p.get(k);
            let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
            while j < es.len() {
                if pr.1 == es[j].a {
                    if pair_uniq(&mut p, pr.0, es[j].b) { grew = true; }
                }
                j = j + 1i64;
            }
            k = k + 1i64;
        }
    }
    return p;
}

// ── ORACLE HALF 2: the CLOSED FORM of the over-delete set. No propagation, no
// delta, no fixpoint over OD — two closures and a double loop over DE. A defect
// in the emitter's propagation has nothing here to be shared with.
fn od_oracle(all: &[E], de: &[E], pres: &[E]) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let cl_all: Vec<(i64, i64)>  = naive_closure(all);
    let cl_pres: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(pres);
    let mut m: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut d: i64 = 0i64;
    while d < de.len() {
        let u: i64 = de[d].a;
        let v: i64 = de[d].b;
        // xs = {u} ∪ { x : (x,u) ∈ closure(ALL) }   — the prefix walk, in ALL.
        let mut xs: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
        xs.push(u);
        let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
        while i < cl_all.len() {
            let c: (i64, i64) = cl_all.get(i);
            if c.1 == u {
                let mut seen: bool = false;
                let mut q: i64 = 0i64;
                while q < xs.len() { if xs.get(q) == c.0 { seen = true; } q = q + 1i64; }
                if !seen { xs.push(c.0); }
            }
            i = i + 1i64;
        }
        // zs = {v} ∪ { z : (v,z) ∈ closure(PRES) }  — the suffix walk, survivors.
        let mut zs: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
        zs.push(v);
        let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
        while j < cl_pres.len() {
            let c2: (i64, i64) = cl_pres.get(j);
            if c2.0 == v {
                let mut seen2: bool = false;
                let mut q2: i64 = 0i64;
                while q2 < zs.len() { if zs.get(q2) == c2.1 { seen2 = true; } q2 = q2 + 1i64; }
                if !seen2 { zs.push(c2.1); }
            }
            j = j + 1i64;
        }
        let mut a: i64 = 0i64;
        while a < xs.len() {
            let mut b: i64 = 0i64;
            while b < zs.len() {
                pair_uniq(&mut m, xs.get(a), zs.get(b));
                b = b + 1i64;
            }
            a = a + 1i64;
        }
        d = d + 1i64;
    }
    return m;
}

// Set equality on Vec<(i64,i64)>: mutual containment PLUS equal length, so a
// duplicate in either side is a disagreement rather than something the
// containment check absorbs.
fn same_set(x: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, y: &Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> bool {
    if x.len() != y.len() { return false; }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < x.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = x.get(i);
        if !pair_has(y, c.0, c.1) { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
    while j < y.len() {
        let c2: (i64, i64) = y.get(j);
        if !pair_has(x, c2.0, c2.1) { return false; }
        j = j + 1i64;
    }
    return true;
}

// ── ORDER, AS A STRUCTURAL PROPERTY. `x` is a SUBSEQUENCE of `y`: the same
// rows in the same relative order, with rows dropped but never reordered. This
// is precisely what a write-index retain preserves and what a rebuild-into-a-
// fresh-Vec would ALSO preserve — so it is not a test of the implementation
// choice, it is the ORDER CONTRACT both choices owe, stated so that a future
// compact that sorts, or swaps-with-last, or uses `remove` from the front, is
// caught. `swap`-with-last compaction is the realistic wrong answer here: it is
// O(|S|) instead of O(|T|) and it SCRAMBLES the order.
fn is_subseq(x: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, y: &Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < x.len() {
        let a: (i64, i64) = x.get(i);
        let mut hit: bool = false;
        while j < y.len() {
            let b: (i64, i64) = y.get(j);
            j = j + 1i64;
            if b.0 == a.0 && b.1 == a.1 { hit = true; break; }
        }
        if !hit { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return true;
}

// Exact row SEQUENCE — equal length and equal element-by-element. `same_set`
// deliberately cannot see order; this deliberately can.
fn seq_eq(x: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, y: &Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> bool {
    if x.len() != y.len() { return false; }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < x.len() {
        let a: (i64, i64) = x.get(i);
        let b: (i64, i64) = y.get(i);
        if a.0 != b.0 || a.1 != b.1 { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return true;
}

fn pair_copy(x: &Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut o: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < x.len() { let c: (i64, i64) = x.get(i); o.push((c.0, c.1)); i = i + 1i64; }
    return o;
}

fn ed(a: i64, b: i64) -> E { return E { a: a, b: b }; }

fn edge_has(v: &Vec<E>, a: i64, b: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < v.len() {
        let e: E = v.get(i);
        if e.a == a && e.b == b { return true; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return false;
}

fn survivors(all: &Vec<E>, de: &Vec<E>) -> Vec<E> {
    let mut out: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < all.len() {
        let e: E = all.get(i);
        if !edge_has(de, e.a, e.b) { out.push(e); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return out;
}

// ── ONE CELL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Build the previous total with the FROM-SCRATCH driver, check it against the
// naive closure, then run PHASE 1a + PHASE 1b and check the over-delete set
// against the closed form. Returns 0, or a code naming which check spoke.
//
// ⚠ THE ASSERTIONS SIT AFTER BOTH FIXPOINTS. On every cell here the broken code
// runs the seed over the whole previous total and then propagates to quiescence
// over the whole survivor graph before anything is looked at; nothing is caught
// on a first push.
fn cell(all: &Vec<E>, de: &Vec<E>, want_od_n: i64) -> i32 {
    let pres: Vec<E> = survivors(all, de);

    // T — the previous total, from the emitter's OWN from-scratch driver, so
    // phase 1 is fed exactly what a maintained handle would hold.
    let mut tot: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0(all.as_slice(), &mut tot) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; }
    }
    let cl_all: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(all.as_slice());
    if !same_set(&tot, &cl_all) { return 2i32; }

    // PHASE 1a — the seed. Edges bound to DE, the rel read from T.
    let mut od: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0_od(de.as_slice(), tot.as_slice(), &mut od) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; }
    }
    // PHASE 1b — propagate along SURVIVOR edges to the OD fixpoint.
    match __wql_tc_scc0_odp(pres.as_slice(), &mut od) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; }
    }

    let want: Vec<(i64, i64)> = od_oracle(all.as_slice(), de.as_slice(), pres.as_slice());
    // The hand-computed cardinality, so a wrong oracle and a wrong engine
    // agreeing is still caught.
    if want.len() != want_od_n { return 5i32; }
    if !same_set(&od, &want)   { return 6i32; }
    // OD ⊆ T — over-delete may never invent a fact the relation did not hold.
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < od.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = od.get(i);
        if !pair_has(&tot, c.0, c.1) { return 7i32; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return 0i32;
}

// ── ONE CELL, PHASE 2 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Runs phases 1a+1b again and then COMPACTS, asserting the INTERMEDIATE SET the
// compact leaves — `S = T ∖ OD` — as a set, as a length, and as an ORDER, plus
// the three latches `_cpt` reports.
//
// ⚠ WHY THE INTERMEDIATE SET AND NOT THE ANSWER. Measured twice on two builds:
// delete phase 1a's `existing-rel ⋈ DE` seed term and the DIAMOND's final answer
// is byte-for-byte correct — same rows, same order — while `od` goes 2→1, `S`
// 3→4 and the re-derive count 1→0. Phase 3 repairs the over-deletion and only
// INCREMENTALITY dies. An answer oracle is blind to this whole arc, so every
// assertion below is about what phase 2 LEAVES, never about what a later phase
// would compute from it.
//
// `S` is derived from the ORACLE's OD (`od_oracle`) and the pre-compact total,
// not from the engine's `od`, so a phase-1 defect cannot cancel against a
// phase-2 defect.
//
// ⚠ THE ASSERTIONS SIT AFTER BOTH FIXPOINTS AND A FULL SCAN OF T. Phases 1a and
// 1b run to quiescence over the whole graph, then `_cpt`'s counting pass walks
// every row of T, before anything is looked at.
fn cell_p2(all: &Vec<E>, de: &Vec<E>, want_s_n: i64) -> i32 {
    let pres: Vec<E> = survivors(all, de);

    let mut tot: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0(all.as_slice(), &mut tot) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; }
    }
    let mut od: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0_od(de.as_slice(), tot.as_slice(), &mut od) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 2i32; }
    }
    match __wql_tc_scc0_odp(pres.as_slice(), &mut od) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; }
    }

    // The total EXACTLY as it stood before the compact — the only thing the
    // order assertion can be made against once `_cpt` has rewritten it in place.
    let t_pre: Vec<(i64, i64)> = pair_copy(&tot);

    // ── PHASE 2 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // `rm` is the NEGATIVE DELTA — the rows the compact removed, `OD ∩ T`, in
    // T's own order. It is an out-param and not a derivable quantity: the
    // compact overwrites the dropped rows in place, so after the call the only
    // copy of them is this Vec (the caller's T has been rewritten and `od` is a
    // SUPERSET that may name rows T never held).
    //
    // ⚠⚠ BOTH OUT-PARAMS ARE SEEDED, AND ON THE **SUCCESS** PATH — WHICH IS THE
    // PATH THAT HAD NO WITNESS. The refusal cell at the bottom of this file
    // seeds them to prove a REFUSAL writes nothing; nothing anywhere proved a
    // SUCCESS clears. Every successful `_cpt` call in this file used to be handed
    // a FRESH Vec, and a fresh Vec passes identically whether the callee clears
    // first or appends to what it was given — MEASURED for `__lat` once already:
    // deleting `__lat.clear()` outright left both fixtures at 0. `555` and (8,8)
    // are out of every legal range here (|OD|, |S| ≥ 0 and small; the chain's
    // rows all have a < b ≤ 4), so their SURVIVAL is a defect and their absence
    // is a fact only a write can produce. `lat.len() != 3` below is what catches
    // the latch; the `same_set` on `rm` is what catches the removed set.
    let mut lat: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    lat.push(555i64);
    let mut rm: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    rm.push((8i64, 8i64));
    match __wql_tc_scc0_cpt(od.as_slice(), &mut tot, &mut rm, &mut lat) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; }
    }

    // THE LATCHES — the emitted counterpart of the interpreter's
    // `od_last`/`cpt_n`/`rdv_n`, three i64 per SCC member and one member here.
    if lat.len() != 3i64          { return 5i32; }
    if lat.get(0i64) != od.len()  { return 6i32; }   // |OD|, distinct
    if lat.get(1i64) != tot.len() { return 7i32; }   // |S|
    // ⚠ −1, NOT 0. Phase 3 does not exist in this slice, and "never ran" is a
    // different state from "ran and re-derived nothing". This assertion is what
    // keeps the ABSENCE of re-derivation observable rather than assumed; the
    // round that adds phase 3 must change this line, deliberately.
    if lat.get(2i64) != 0i64 - 1i64 { return 8i32; }

    // ── S AS A SET, against the ORACLE's OD over the pre-compact total ────
    let want_od: Vec<(i64, i64)> = od_oracle(all.as_slice(), de.as_slice(), pres.as_slice());
    let mut want_s: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut si: i64 = 0i64;
    while si < t_pre.len() {
        let cc: (i64, i64) = t_pre.get(si);
        if !pair_has(&want_od, cc.0, cc.1) { pair_uniq(&mut want_s, cc.0, cc.1); }
        si = si + 1i64;
    }
    if want_s.len() != want_s_n { return 9i32; }     // hand-computed cardinality
    if !same_set(&tot, &want_s) { return 10i32; }    // set equality, dup-sensitive

    // ── S AS AN ORDER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // In-place retain keeps the survivors' RELATIVE order, so `S` is a
    // subsequence of the pre-compact total. Dropping `_cpt`'s `pop` loop leaves
    // the stale tail and breaks the LENGTH first; scrambling (a swap-with-last
    // compaction) breaks this and nothing else.
    if !is_subseq(&tot, &t_pre) { return 11i32; }

    // ── THE NEGATIVE DELTA `rm`, AS A SET, A COUNT AND AN ORDER ───────────
    // ⚠ WHY THIS IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH `|OD|` AND `|S|`. The two latches are
    // COUNTS; a fold that has to undo the removal needs the ROWS. And `rm` is
    // not recoverable from anything else the call leaves behind: `t_pre` here is
    // a fixture-side copy that a real driver does not have, `tot` has been
    // rewritten in place, and `od` is a strict SUPERSET of `OD ∩ T` in the
    // general case (the refusal cell below is exactly such a case).
    //
    // The oracle is `want_od` — the CLOSED FORM's OD — intersected with the
    // pre-compact total, so a phase-1 defect cannot cancel against a phase-2 one.
    let mut want_rm: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut ri: i64 = 0i64;
    while ri < t_pre.len() {
        let rr: (i64, i64) = t_pre.get(ri);
        if pair_has(&want_od, rr.0, rr.1) { pair_uniq(&mut want_rm, rr.0, rr.1); }
        ri = ri + 1i64;
    }
    // The seed is GONE — a successful compact CLEARS `__rm_<m>` rather than
    // appending to whatever the caller was carrying. Named separately from the
    // set equality so the failure says which of the two things broke.
    if pair_has(&rm, 8i64, 8i64) { return 16i32; }
    if !same_set(&rm, &want_rm) { return 12i32; }
    // S and RM PARTITION the pre-compact total: every row went to exactly one
    // side. Stated as arithmetic AND as disjointness, because a `rm` that
    // duplicated a row while dropping another would satisfy the count alone.
    if rm.len() + tot.len() != t_pre.len() { return 13i32; }
    let mut di: i64 = 0i64;
    while di < rm.len() {
        let dc: (i64, i64) = rm.get(di);
        if pair_has(&tot, dc.0, dc.1) { return 14i32; }
        di = di + 1i64;
    }
    // …and `rm` carries T's ORDER, not OD's propagation order — a subsequence
    // of the pre-compact total, the same contract `S` owes.
    if !is_subseq(&rm, &t_pre) { return 15i32; }
    return 0i32;
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    // ══ CELL 1 — THE CHAIN. UNDER-DELETION, at DEPTH. ════════════════════
    // ALL = 1→2, 2→3, 3→4;  DE = 2→3;  PRES = 1→2, 3→4.
    // T          = {12,23,34,13,24,14}                       — 6 rows
    // closure(PRES) = {12,34}
    // OD (by hand): xs = {2} ∪ {x:(x,2)∈T} = {2,1};
    //               zs = {3} ∪ {z:(3,z)∈closure(PRES)} = {3,4}
    //             = {(2,3),(2,4),(1,3),(1,4)}                — 4 rows
    // T ∖ OD = {12,34} = closure(PRES): here compact ALONE is already right.
    // ⚠ (1,4) IS THE CELL. It is derived THROUGH the deleted edge at depth 2,
    // never FROM it, so a seed missing the `existing-rel ⋈ DE` term over-deletes
    // {(2,3),(2,4)} only and leaves (1,3),(1,4) standing — the answer after
    // compact would be too BIG.
    let mut a1: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    a1.push(ed(1i64, 2i64)); a1.push(ed(2i64, 3i64)); a1.push(ed(3i64, 4i64));
    let mut d1: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d1.push(ed(2i64, 3i64));
    let r1: i32 = cell(&a1, &d1, 4i64);
    if r1 != 0i32 { return 30i32 + r1; }

    // ══ CELL 2 — THE DIAMOND. OVER-DELETION IS REAL. ═════════════════════
    // ALL = 1→2, 1→3, 2→4, 3→4;  DE = 2→4;  PRES = 1→2, 1→3, 3→4.
    // T = {12,13,24,34,14}                                   — 5 rows
    //     (1,4) has TWO derivations: 1→2→4 and 1→3→4.
    // closure(PRES) = {12,13,34,14}                          — (1,4) SURVIVES
    // OD (by hand): xs = {2} ∪ {x:(x,2)∈T} = {2,1};
    //               zs = {4} ∪ {z:(4,z)∈closure(PRES)} = {4}
    //             = {(2,4),(1,4)}                            — 2 rows
    let mut a2: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    a2.push(ed(1i64, 2i64)); a2.push(ed(1i64, 3i64));
    a2.push(ed(2i64, 4i64)); a2.push(ed(3i64, 4i64));
    let mut d2: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d2.push(ed(2i64, 4i64));
    let r2: i32 = cell(&a2, &d2, 2i64);
    if r2 != 0i32 { return 40i32 + r2; }

    // ── AND THE STRICTNESS, ASSERTED BY NAME. This is the sentence that makes
    // phase 3 necessary rather than decorative, and it is the reason `OD` is NOT
    // `closure(ALL) ∖ closure(PRES)`: (1,4) is over-deleted in phase 1 AND is in
    // the correct final answer. Whatever phase 2 removes, phase 3 must put this
    // row back. If this ever stops holding, either phase 1 stopped
    // over-deleting (and the fixture below it is measuring a weaker engine) or
    // the diamond stopped being a diamond.
    let mut p2: Vec<E> = survivors(&a2, &d2);
    let od2_want: Vec<(i64, i64)> = od_oracle(a2.as_slice(), d2.as_slice(), p2.as_slice());
    let cl_p2: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(p2.as_slice());
    if !pair_has(&od2_want, 1i64, 4i64) { return 60i32; }   // over-deleted …
    if !pair_has(&cl_p2, 1i64, 4i64)    { return 61i32; }   // … and still TRUE.
    // The same fact, straight out of the ENGINE rather than the oracle.
    let mut tot2: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0(a2.as_slice(), &mut tot2) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 62i32; }
    }
    let mut od2: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0_od(d2.as_slice(), tot2.as_slice(), &mut od2) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 63i32; }
    }
    match __wql_tc_scc0_odp(p2.as_slice(), &mut od2) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 64i32; }
    }
    if !pair_has(&od2, 1i64, 4i64) { return 65i32; }

    // ══ CELL 3 — THE SELF-SUPPORTING CYCLE. THE ONE COUNTING CANNOT PASS. ══
    // ALL = 0→1, 1→2, 2→3, 3→1;  DE = 1→2 (a CYCLE edge);
    // PRES = 0→1, 2→3, 3→1.
    // T = {(0,1),(0,2),(0,3)} ∪ {1,2,3}×{1,2,3}              — 12 rows,
    //     INCLUDING the self-pairs (1,1),(2,2),(3,3): every member of the cycle
    //     supports itself THROUGH the cycle.
    // closure(PRES) = {(0,1),(2,3),(2,1),(3,1)}              — 4 rows
    // OD (by hand): xs = {1} ∪ {x:(x,1)∈T} = {1,0,2,3};
    //               zs = {2} ∪ {z:(2,z)∈closure(PRES)} = {2,3,1}
    //             = {0,1,2,3}×{1,2,3}                        — 12 rows = ALL OF T.
    // Phase 1 therefore over-deletes the ENTIRE relation and phase 3 must
    // re-derive all four survivors. Under any multiplicity/counting scheme the
    // nine pairs among {1,2,3} keep a positive weight forever and NOTHING is
    // deleted — the cell that separates DRed from counting.
    // ⚠ It also forces phase 1b to walk the cycle: the seed alone does not
    // contain (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) by any one-round argument.
    let mut a3: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    a3.push(ed(0i64, 1i64)); a3.push(ed(1i64, 2i64));
    a3.push(ed(2i64, 3i64)); a3.push(ed(3i64, 1i64));
    let mut d3: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d3.push(ed(1i64, 2i64));
    let r3: i32 = cell(&a3, &d3, 12i64);
    if r3 != 0i32 { return 50i32 + r3; }

    // T ∖ OD is EMPTY here, stated as its own assertion because it is the
    // strongest form of "phase 2 alone is not an answer".
    let mut tot3: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0(a3.as_slice(), &mut tot3) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 70i32; }
    }
    if tot3.len() != 12i64 { return 71i32; }
    let mut p3: Vec<E> = survivors(&a3, &d3);
    let mut od3: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0_od(d3.as_slice(), tot3.as_slice(), &mut od3) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 72i32; }
    }
    match __wql_tc_scc0_odp(p3.as_slice(), &mut od3) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 73i32; }
    }
    if od3.len() != 12i64 { return 74i32; }
    let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
    while k < tot3.len() {
        let c3: (i64, i64) = tot3.get(k);
        if !pair_has(&od3, c3.0, c3.1) { return 75i32; }
        k = k + 1i64;
    }
    // …and the four survivor facts are ALL inside OD, so re-derivation is the
    // only thing that can produce the correct answer for this graph.
    let cl_p3: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(p3.as_slice());
    if cl_p3.len() != 4i64 { return 76i32; }
    let mut q: i64 = 0i64;
    while q < cl_p3.len() {
        let c4: (i64, i64) = cl_p3.get(q);
        if !pair_has(&od3, c4.0, c4.1) { return 77i32; }
        q = q + 1i64;
    }

    // ══ PHASE 2 — THE COMPACT, ON THE SAME THREE GRAPHS ══════════════════
    // T ∖ OD by hand, from the OD sets computed above:
    //   CHAIN   T={12,23,34,13,24,14}, OD={23,24,13,14}      → S={12,34}    2
    //   DIAMOND T={12,13,24,34,14},    OD={24,14}            → S={12,13,34} 3
    //   CYCLE   T = OD (12 rows)                             → S={}         0
    // ⚠ THE CYCLE'S S IS EMPTY AND THAT IS THE POINT: phase 2 alone answers
    // NOTHING there, and the four facts `closure(PRES)` still holds are all
    // inside OD. Phase 3 is the only thing that can put them back.
    let r1c: i32 = cell_p2(&a1, &d1, 2i64);
    if r1c != 0i32 { return 100i32 + r1c; }
    let r2c: i32 = cell_p2(&a2, &d2, 3i64);
    if r2c != 0i32 { return 120i32 + r2c; }
    let r3c: i32 = cell_p2(&a3, &d3, 0i64);
    if r3c != 0i32 { return 140i32 + r3c; }

    // ══ THE ORDER, PINNED AS AN EXACT SEQUENCE ═══════════════════════════
    // `cell_p2` asserts S is a SUBSEQUENCE of the pre-compact total, which is
    // the contract both a retain and a rebuild owe. Here the two sequences
    // themselves are written down, because the moment phase 3 exists nothing
    // can distinguish "phase 2 preserved the order" from "phase 3 happened to
    // append in the right place".
    //
    // The pre-compact order is the emitter's FIRST-DERIVATION order: round 0
    // runs the base body over `es` in input order, then each semi-naive round
    // appends what that round newly derived. For the chain that is
    // [12,23,34] then [13,24] then [14]; for the diamond [12,13,24,34] then
    // [14] — (1,4) is derived twice in the same round and the shadow set keeps
    // the first. IN-PLACE RETAIN then cuts the over-deleted rows out and leaves
    // every survivor where it was.
    let mut tt1: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0(a1.as_slice(), &mut tt1) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 80i32; }
    }
    let mut want_t1: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    want_t1.push((1i64, 2i64)); want_t1.push((2i64, 3i64)); want_t1.push((3i64, 4i64));
    want_t1.push((1i64, 3i64)); want_t1.push((2i64, 4i64)); want_t1.push((1i64, 4i64));
    if !seq_eq(&tt1, &want_t1) { return 81i32; }
    let p1: Vec<E> = survivors(&a1, &d1);
    let mut od1: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0_od(d1.as_slice(), tt1.as_slice(), &mut od1) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 82i32; }
    }
    match __wql_tc_scc0_odp(p1.as_slice(), &mut od1) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 83i32; }
    }
    let mut lat1: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut rm1: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0_cpt(od1.as_slice(), &mut tt1, &mut rm1, &mut lat1) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 84i32; }
    }
    let mut want_s1: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    want_s1.push((1i64, 2i64)); want_s1.push((3i64, 4i64));
    if !seq_eq(&tt1, &want_s1) { return 85i32; }
    // THE REMOVED ROWS, AS AN EXACT SEQUENCE. T = [12,23,34,13,24,14] and
    // OD = {23,13,24,14}, so T's order restricted to OD is [23,13,24,14] —
    // NOT OD's own propagation order, which puts the seed rows {23,13} first
    // and then {24,14} and happens to agree here only by accident of the graph.
    // The diamond below is where the two orders actually differ.
    let mut want_rm1: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    want_rm1.push((2i64, 3i64)); want_rm1.push((1i64, 3i64));
    want_rm1.push((2i64, 4i64)); want_rm1.push((1i64, 4i64));
    if !seq_eq(&rm1, &want_rm1) { return 160i32; }

    let mut tt2: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0(a2.as_slice(), &mut tt2) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 86i32; }
    }
    let mut want_t2: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    want_t2.push((1i64, 2i64)); want_t2.push((1i64, 3i64)); want_t2.push((2i64, 4i64));
    want_t2.push((3i64, 4i64)); want_t2.push((1i64, 4i64));
    if !seq_eq(&tt2, &want_t2) { return 87i32; }
    let mut od2b: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0_od(d2.as_slice(), tt2.as_slice(), &mut od2b) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 88i32; }
    }
    match __wql_tc_scc0_odp(p2.as_slice(), &mut od2b) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 89i32; }
    }
    let mut lat2: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut rm2: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0_cpt(od2b.as_slice(), &mut tt2, &mut rm2, &mut lat2) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 90i32; }
    }
    let mut want_s2: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    want_s2.push((1i64, 2i64)); want_s2.push((1i64, 3i64)); want_s2.push((3i64, 4i64));
    if !seq_eq(&tt2, &want_s2) { return 91i32; }
    // T = [12,13,24,34,14], OD = {24,14} ⇒ rm = [24,14] in T's order.
    let mut want_rm2: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    want_rm2.push((2i64, 4i64)); want_rm2.push((1i64, 4i64));
    if !seq_eq(&rm2, &want_rm2) { return 161i32; }
    // ⚠ AND THE DIAMOND'S `rm` IS EXACTLY WHAT PHASE 3 MUST PARTLY UNDO: (1,4)
    // is in it AND is still true over PRES. A fold that took `rm` as the final
    // negative delta without waiting for the re-derive would be wrong here, on
    // this row, which is why the phase-3 fixture asserts `RD ⊆ rm`.
    if !pair_has(&rm2, 1i64, 4i64) { return 162i32; }

    // ══ `OD ⊆ T` — THE REFUSAL, AND THE STATE IT LEAVES ══════════════════
    // Nothing in the tree checks `DE ⊆ EDB`: there is no retraction surface to
    // check it at, so a caller cannot present a bad DE today. Phase 2 is
    // nevertheless where the violation would FIRST become visible — as a
    // survivor count that does not add up — and it refuses there.
    //
    // DE = {2→3, 9→9} over the CHAIN. 9→9 was never inserted, so `_od`'s BASE
    // body seeds OD with (9,9), a fact the total never held:
    //   OD = {23,13} (seed) ∪ {24,14} (propagate) ∪ {99}   — 5 rows
    //   T ∖ OD = {12,34}                                   — 2 rows
    //   6 − 2 = 4 ≠ 5   ⇒  Err(RetractAbsent)
    //
    // ⚠⚠ THIS REFUSAL IS EXPENSIVE, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE STATE ASSERTION
    // BELOW WORTH MAKING. Four of the five OD rows ARE in the total, so a
    // one-pass implementation — retain first, discover the mismatch after —
    // would already have rewritten the front of the Vec and popped it to two
    // rows before it could refuse, and those rows are unrecoverable. The
    // assertion is that the total is unchanged in CONTENT and in ORDER, i.e.
    // that the compact is TRANSACTIONAL. A DE of ONLY {9→9} would make the same
    // assertion pass FOR FREE — no row of T is in OD, so even the broken form
    // writes each row back onto itself — which is exactly the cheap refusal that
    // proves nothing, and is why this cell deletes a REAL edge alongside.
    let mut tt3: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0(a1.as_slice(), &mut tt3) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 92i32; }
    }
    let mut dbad: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    dbad.push(ed(2i64, 3i64)); dbad.push(ed(9i64, 9i64));
    let pbad: Vec<E> = survivors(&a1, &dbad);
    let mut odbad: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    match __wql_tc_scc0_od(dbad.as_slice(), tt3.as_slice(), &mut odbad) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 93i32; }
    }
    match __wql_tc_scc0_odp(pbad.as_slice(), &mut odbad) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 94i32; }
    }
    if odbad.len() != 5i64 { return 95i32; }
    if !pair_has(&odbad, 9i64, 9i64) { return 96i32; }   // the fact T never held
    if pair_has(&tt3, 9i64, 9i64)    { return 97i32; }
    // ⚠ SEEDED, NOT FRESH — AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS BINDING.
    // Asked of a FRESH empty Vec this block passes identically whether the
    // emitter clears the latch, writes it, or does neither: MEASURED, deleting
    // `__lat.clear()` outright left this fixture and its sibling at 0. A latch
    // seeded with a value the callee never writes is the only input on which
    // "a refused compact does not touch the caller's latch" is a claim at all.
    // 777 is arbitrary and out of every latch's range (|OD|, |S| >= 0, |RD| = -1).
    let mut latbad: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    latbad.push(777i64);
    // ⚠ SEEDED OUT OF RANGE, FOR THE SAME REASON AND ONE MORE. `rm` is a Vec the
    // callee would leave alone on a refusal whether it is transactional or
    // merely lazy, so asked of a FRESH Vec the assertion below is vacuous. (7,7)
    // is a pair the chain never derives — every row of T here has a < b — so
    // "unchanged" is a claim about a value only a WRITE could remove. On the
    // successful path the callee CLEARS `__rm_<m>`, so this seed is also the only
    // input that separates "cleared then filled" from "appended to whatever the
    // caller had": the successful cells above start from a fresh Vec and cannot.
    let mut rmbad: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    rmbad.push((7i64, 7i64));
    match __wql_tc_scc0_cpt(odbad.as_slice(), &mut tt3, &mut rmbad, &mut latbad) {
        Result::Ok(_u) => { return 98i32; }              // it must NOT succeed
        Result::Err(_e) => { }
    }
    // THE NEGATIVE DELTA, UNTOUCHED — the refusal is transactional in `rm` too.
    // Four of the five OD rows ARE in T, so a one-pass compact would have pushed
    // four rows here before it could refuse, and a clear-before-check would have
    // dropped the seed.
    if rmbad.len() != 1i64 { return 163i32; }
    if !pair_has(&rmbad, 7i64, 7i64) { return 164i32; }
    // THE TOTAL, UNTOUCHED — same rows, same order as before the refused call.
    if !seq_eq(&tt3, &want_t1) { return 99i32; }
    // …and no latch was written: a refused compact reports nothing, so a reader
    // cannot mistake a stale latch for a completed phase.
    // The refusal is TRANSACTIONAL IN THE LATCH TOO, not merely in the totals:
    // the seed must survive untouched. An emitter that clears before checking
    // answers len()==0 here and destroys a report the caller cannot rebuild.
    if latbad.len() != 1i64   { return 79i32; }
    if latbad.get(0i64) != 777i64 { return 78i32; }

    return 0i32;
}
